// Protocol Buffers schema for the felis terminal emulator's IPC.
//
// This file is the single machine-readable authority for the wire: the
// 10 message families, their supporting structs/enums, and the frame
// kinds that select them. A family whose arms travel one way is one
// wrapper, `<Family>Msg`; a family with arms in both directions is two,
// `<Family>ToDaemonMsg` and `<Family>ToClientMsg`, under the one frame
// kind, so no wrapper can carry an arm the wrong way.
// `crates/felis-protocol/src/messages.rs` holds the Rust *domain* types
// the workspace programs against (validated constructors over the same
// shapes, not a second contract), and `convert/` is the validator
// between them, checking what the schema cannot state (a required oneof,
// an enum with no `UNSPECIFIED` counterpart, an integer that must
// narrow). Where the two could disagree the schema wins, and the tests
// that read this file (`the_protocol_version_matches_the_schema`,
// `every_message_kind_matches_the_schema_enum`) are what keeps them from
// drifting apart quietly. Field numbers inside each family's `oneof` are
// frozen as declared here for the life of a protocol major, and a
// two-wrapper family numbers its arms once across both wrappers: a body
// decoded as the wrong half then names no arm, rather than a different
// one. A new arm takes the next free number in its family, whatever its
// Rust variant order. A number the release baseline (the newest
// published final release) carries is `reserved` on retirement and
// never recycled, so a released peer's bytes can never be read as a new
// field; a number retired while no baseline exists is free again, since
// the `base:` acknowledgment that permits its retirement already
// requires both sides to be rebuilt together (docs/explanation/architecture/ipc.md "Schema evolution:
// major, minor, feature flag").
//
// Versioning happens in the frozen preface, not in this schema. Before
// any frame, a client sends 8 bytes: magic "FLIS" (0x46 0x4C 0x49 0x53),
// protocol major (uint16), protocol minor (uint16). The daemon answers
// with 10: magic, a status word (0 = accept, 1 = refuse, anything
// else = refuse for a reason this peer cannot name), and two words whose
// meaning the status picks (accept: agreed major + daemon minor; refuse:
// the supported major range, min then max). An accept echoes the major
// the client offered, and a client closes on an accept naming any other
// major before it reads a frame. Every preface integer is
// big-endian, unlike the little-endian frame header. A major names
// *this* schema; a minor names an additive revision of it, and the
// effective minor, min(client, daemon), is what a sender may use.
//
// The constants this schema is authoritative for, in the exact form
// `felis-protocol/src/preface.rs` parses out of this file (its
// `the_protocol_version_matches_the_schema` test reads the two lines
// below and asserts the Rust values equal them, so editing one here
// without the other fails the build; prose-check: ignore):
//    PROTOCOL_MAJOR = 1
//    PROTOCOL_MINOR = 0
//
// Kind or arm: a frame kind is a *surface*, so a message addressing a
// surface no existing kind owns earns a kind, as does one whose
// refusal must land before its body is decoded. Another request,
// reply, or item shape on a surface that already exists is a new
// `oneof` arm of that family, whatever correlation class it needs.
// `Ops::Status` is an arm, `Search` is a kind
// (docs/explanation/architecture/ipc.md "Kind or arm?").
//
// Because the arm is the routing unit, every arm below carries an
// `(felis.v1.arm)` field option stating its whole row of the routing
// table, in this exact form:
//
//    [(felis.v1.arm) = {
//      direction: <DIRECTION_TO_DAEMON | DIRECTION_TO_CLIENT>
//      correlation: <CORRELATION_*>
//      modes: <MODE_*>      // repeated, in WINDOW, OPS, OBSERVER order
//      phases: <PHASE_*>    // repeated, in ladder order
//      since: <minor>       // omitted where it is 0
//    }]
//
// where direction is the one way the arm may travel (the direction its
// wrapper travels, restated so the row stands alone); correlation is
// the envelope it demands; modes names the connection modes on which
// the arm is legal, receive side; phases names the connection states
// it may arrive in, where `setup` is a welcomed connection that has
// not yet said what it is for, `attached` one subscribed to a session,
// and `observing` one serving a notification stream; and since is the
// protocol minor that introduced the arm, written only where it is not
// 0: this major's base schema is minor 0, which is the proto3 default
// and absent from the descriptor whether or not it is spelled, so an
// addition is the one place a `since` appears. The `ArmRouting`
// message below names the vocabulary. A peer
// that cannot read Rust reads these options out of the descriptor this
// file compiles to (`buf build`, or protoc reflection), with no comment
// to parse; the Rust side asserts them against its own table
// (`the_schema_declares_the_same_arm_table`), so an arm added here
// without an option, or with the wrong one, fails the build. Options
// are invisible to `buf breaking`, so that test, not the compat gate,
// is what catches a routing edit.
//
// Naming: a message that is a `oneof` arm of a family wrapper is named
// `<Family><Variant>` (the family being the wrapper minus `Msg`,
// `ToDaemonMsg`, or `ToClientMsg`), since proto has one flat package
// scope and a bare `Region` or `Event` would squat it. Shared vocabulary (anything two families reference, or that
// appears as a field type rather than an arm) keeps its bare name.
//
// Correlation rides *inside* the body, never in the frame header: field
// **100** of every wrapper is reserved wire-wide for the `Correlation`
// envelope, set on the families that carry requests, replies, and
// stream items and `reserved` on the ones that do not. The slot is uniform so a reader can lift the envelope off any body with
// one field number and no per-family table, which is what lets the
// shared connection driver validate correlation for every family in one
// place. It is an optional message field: absent costs zero bytes, so
// the un-correlated connection-scoped pushes (Grid / Image / Push /
// Input) are byte-for-byte what they were before correlation existed.
//
// The `felis.v1` package is a schema-namespace major, and it moves only
// when a daemon has to hold two schemas at once: a deprecation window
// serves the prior and the new major side by side, which needs both
// message sets in one binary: exactly what a `felis.v2` package
// provides. It does not track `PROTOCOL_MAJOR` bump for bump: before
// the compatibility freeze the dev wire carries no promise and breaks
// ride major 1 unbumped, so the package moves only with a post-freeze
// major whose deprecation window needs both message sets. Minor growth
// stays inside the package either way.
//
// Type-mapping notes that recur below:
//    * Session ids are Rust `u128`; proto3 has no 128-bit integer, so they
//      travel as `bytes` (16 bytes, big-endian). Inlined rather than
//      wrapped in a message: the wrapper would buy nothing a field
//      comment does not, and would cost a nested allocation per id.
//    * `RowPayload` is opaque cell bytes → `bytes` (the inner row codec's
//      output; `docs/reference/row-codec.md` is the authority for the
//      interior of that one field; see `RowEntry.packed_cells`).
//    * `u8`/`u16`/`u32` → `uint32`; `u64` → `uint64`. Deliberately-signed
//      ints use zigzag (`sint32`/`sint64`).
//    * bitflags (`InputMods: u8`, `KittyKbdFlags: u8`) carry raw bits in
//      a `uint32`; see `messages.rs` / `kitty_keyboard.rs` for the bits.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FrameKind (the frame header's `kind` word, from lib.rs)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Discriminator in the frame header's little-endian kind word. Never a
/// field type; kind 0 is CONN because wire values are frozen for major 1.
/// buf:lint:ignore ENUM_ZERO_VALUE_SUFFIX
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, PartialOrd, Ord, ::prost::Enumeration)]
#[repr(i32)]
pub enum FrameKind {
    /// The connection handshake (ConnToDaemonMsg, ConnToClientMsg).
    Conn = 0,
    /// Client -> daemon: keys, paste, mouse, resize, focus.
    Input = 1,
    /// Daemon -> client: cell diffs, cursor, OSC events.
    Grid = 2,
    /// Daemon -> client: image bytes + placement (Kitty graphics).
    Image = 3,
    /// This connection's session lifecycle (SessionToDaemonMsg,
    /// SessionToClientMsg).
    Session = 4,
    /// Request/reply ops on named other sessions (OpsToDaemonMsg,
    /// OpsToClientMsg).
    Ops = 5,
    /// Region-export family (RegionToDaemonMsg, RegionToClientMsg).
    Region = 6,
    /// Notification observer connections (NotifyToDaemonMsg,
    /// NotifyToClientMsg).
    Notify = 7,
    /// Daemon -> client pushes (PushMsg).
    Push = 8,
    /// Scrollback search conversation on the attached connection
    /// (SearchToDaemonMsg, SearchToClientMsg).
    Search = 9,
}
impl FrameKind {
    /// String value of the enum field names used in the ProtoBuf definition.
    ///
    /// The values are not transformed in any way and thus are considered stable
    /// (if the ProtoBuf definition does not change) and safe for programmatic use.
    pub fn as_str_name(&self) -> &'static str {
        match self {
            Self::Conn => "FRAME_KIND_CONN",
            Self::Input => "FRAME_KIND_INPUT",
            Self::Grid => "FRAME_KIND_GRID",
            Self::Image => "FRAME_KIND_IMAGE",
            Self::Session => "FRAME_KIND_SESSION",
            Self::Ops => "FRAME_KIND_OPS",
            Self::Region => "FRAME_KIND_REGION",
            Self::Notify => "FRAME_KIND_NOTIFY",
            Self::Push => "FRAME_KIND_PUSH",
            Self::Search => "FRAME_KIND_SEARCH",
        }
    }
    /// Creates an enum from field names used in the ProtoBuf definition.
    pub fn from_str_name(value: &str) -> ::core::option::Option<Self> {
        match value {
            "FRAME_KIND_CONN" => Some(Self::Conn),
            "FRAME_KIND_INPUT" => Some(Self::Input),
            "FRAME_KIND_GRID" => Some(Self::Grid),
            "FRAME_KIND_IMAGE" => Some(Self::Image),
            "FRAME_KIND_SESSION" => Some(Self::Session),
            "FRAME_KIND_OPS" => Some(Self::Ops),
            "FRAME_KIND_REGION" => Some(Self::Region),
            "FRAME_KIND_NOTIFY" => Some(Self::Notify),
            "FRAME_KIND_PUSH" => Some(Self::Push),
            "FRAME_KIND_SEARCH" => Some(Self::Search),
            _ => None,
        }
    }
}
